Part B: Lab (75-90 min)

Step 1: Observe default networking

bash
-----------------------
docker network ls
docker run -d --name c1 alpine sleep 3600
docker run -d --name c2 alpine sleep 3600
docker inspect -f '{{.NetworkSettings.IPAddress}}' c1 c2
docker exec c1 ping -c 2 <IP-of-c2>      # works by IP
docker exec c1 ping -c 2 c2              # fails: bad address
-----------------------
Check the host side: ip addr show docker0 and ip route.

Step 2: Custom network with name resolution

bash
---------------------
docker rm -f c1 c2
docker network create labnet
docker run -d --name c1 --network labnet alpine sleep 3600
docker run -d --name c2 --network labnet alpine sleep 3600
docker exec c1 ping -c 2 c2              # works by name now
docker exec c1 cat /etc/resolv.conf       # Docker's embedded DNS at 127.0.0.11
------------------------------------------------
Step 3: A two-tier app (web + database)

bash
-------
docker rm -f c1 c2
docker run -d --name db --network labnet -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=secret postgres:16
docker run -d --name web --network labnet -p 5000:5000 YOURUSER/myapp:v1
docker exec -it db psql -U postgres -c "select version();"
docker exec web python -c "import socket; print(socket.gethostbyname('db'))"
---------------------------------------------------
The web container finds the database by the name db. No IPs hard-coded.

Step 4: See the data loss problem

bash
-------------
docker exec -it db psql -U postgres -c "create table notes(id int, txt text); insert into notes values (1,'hello');"
docker exec -it db psql -U postgres -c "select * from notes;"
docker rm -f db
docker run -d --name db --network labnet -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=secret postgres:16
docker exec -it db psql -U postgres -c "select * from notes;"    # error: table doesn't exist
-------------------------------------------
The data died with the container (the Day 2 writable-layer lesson).

Step 5: Fix it with a named volume

bash
---------
docker rm -f db
docker volume create pgdata
docker run -d --name db --network labnet -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=secret \
  -v pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data postgres:16
sleep 5
docker exec -it db psql -U postgres -c "create table notes(id int, txt text); insert into notes values (1,'hello');"
docker rm -f db
docker run -d --name db --network labnet -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=secret \
  -v pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data postgres:16
sleep 5
docker exec -it db psql -U postgres -c "select * from notes;"     # data survived
---------------------------------------------------------------------
Inspect it: docker volume inspect pgdata and sudo ls /var/lib/docker/volumes/pgdata/_data.

Step 6: Bind mount for live editing

bash
-------------
mkdir -p ~/k8s-journey/html && echo "<h1>Hello from the host</h1>" > ~/k8s-journey/html/index.html
docker run -d --name site -p 8081:80 -v ~/k8s-journey/html:/usr/share/nginx/html:ro nginx
curl localhost:8081
echo "<h1>Edited live</h1>" > ~/k8s-journey/html/index.html
curl localhost:8081                      # changed without restarting
--------------------------------------
Step 7: See the iptables magic behind -p

bash
-----------
sudo iptables -t nat -L DOCKER -n

Find the DNAT rule for ports 5000 and 8081. Kubernetes kube-proxy uses the same mechanism for Services.
